WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.600
The narration is a synthetic voice.

2
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.675
NyayaSiddhi is a test. It asks one question about Indian criminal law.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.405
Each item is the story of an order. The court either established the offence or it did not.

4
00:00:14.405 --> 00:00:18.800
The test checks whether a system says "supported" where the court said no.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.964
A row has two numbers, and they are read together.

6
00:00:24.964 --> 00:00:29.410
One counts items the court rejected that the system still called supported.

7
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.034
The other counts items the court established that the system called supported.

8
00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:35.575
Each comes with its range.

9
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.241
Every row says what it was run on, who ran it, and whether we built it.

10
00:00:48.241 --> 00:00:52.900
We have seen both released sets, so every figure here is a development figure.

11
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.492
Anyone can enter a system. Today only we have.

12
00:00:57.492 --> 00:01:01.055
The route is open, and has been tried only as a local

13
00:01:01.055 --> 00:01:04.550
dry run with a synthetic file; no outside entry yet.

14
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.707
Download the two released sets and check their fingerprints.

15
00:01:09.707 --> 00:01:13.433
Write one prediction file for one set. Write a short manifest.

16
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:16.679
Put both files in your own folder on the dataset page.

17
00:01:16.679 --> 00:01:19.745
Run our checker on your machine until it says pass.

18
00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:22.450
Then open a pull request on the dataset page.

19
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.268
We host this, and we also enter. So the scoring rules are written before anyone enters,

20
00:01:32.268 --> 00:01:36.128
and every row is scored by the same script, with the same ranges.

21
00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:38.029
Our own rows are marked as ours.

22
00:01:38.029 --> 00:01:43.375
We plan for an outside party to be able to re-score a row, and that row would then say so.

23
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.251
Today the board lists rows in a fixed order, by set and system name, never by a figure.

24
00:01:52.251 --> 00:01:55.980
You can look at each offence group and at each way of deciding.

25
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.875
The board does not rank systems.

26
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.325
Later, you will be able to open a row and pick a version.

27
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.875
Today the board holds our own rows. There is no hidden set yet.

28
00:02:14.975 --> 00:02:19.150
There is no hidden set yet. The mechanism for one is planned.
The organisers would hold it; there is no independent custodian yet.


29
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.200
The narration is a synthetic voice.
